      "reasoning": "Let me build the game. I'll write it in parts. First the HTML/CSS skeleton, then the JS physics and game logic.\n\nLet me think about the design of Russian billiards (Русский бильярд / \"Русская пирамида\"):\n- 15 balls numbered 1-15, plus a white cue ball (белый).\n- Balls 1-7 are \"красные\" (red), 9-15 are \"чёрные\" (black), ball 8 is \"свободный\" (free) — can be played by either player.\n- Actually in Russian pyramid: balls 1-7 red, 8 free, 9-15 black.\n- Players are assigned a color (red or black) after the first pot.\n- The cue ball (white) is placed behind the head string (за линией) after a foul or at the start.\n- \"Снюх\" (snooker) — if you hit the wrong color first, it's a foul.\n- Foul: hitting wrong color first, cue ball potted, no ball hit, etc.\n- Win: pot all balls of your color (and the free ball 8).\n\nLet me keep the rules reasonably authentic but playable. I'll implement:\n- Break: 15 balls in triangle, cue ball behind head string.\n- Players alternate; after first legal pot, player gets that color.\n- If you pot your color, you continue.\n- Foul: opponent gets ball in hand (cue ball placement behind head string, or anywhere — in Russian it's \"штрафной удар\" with cue ball behind the line).\n- Potting the cue ball = foul.\n- Potting opponent's color = it stays potted (in Russian billiards, potted balls stay down).\n- Win when you pot your last ball.\n\nLet me implement a solid version. I'll keep it clean.\n\nLet me write the HTML file. I'll do it in chunks using write then append.\n\nLet me design the canvas: table with green felt, 6 pockets. Standard ratio ~2:1. I'll use a canvas of, say, 1000x520 for the play area plus rails.\n\nLet me define:\n- Canvas width 1000, height 540.\n- Table play area with cushions.\n- 6 pockets at corners and middle of long sides.\n\nPhysics:\n- Each ball: x, y, vx, vy, radius, color, number, potted.\n- Friction: multiply velocity by ~0.985 each frame, stop below threshold.\n- Ball-ball collision: elastic, equal mass.\n- Cushion collision: reflect with restitution.\n- Pocket detection: if ball center within pocket radius, potted.\n\nAiming:\n- Mouse to aim direction from cue ball.\n- Power: hold and drag back, or a slider, or click-drag. I'll do: move mouse to aim, press and hold to charge power (or drag distance). Simpler: click and drag from cue ball — the drag vector sets direction and power. Actually a common approach: aim with mouse position (line from cue ball to mouse), then click to shoot with power based on... Let me do a power meter: hold mouse button to increase power, release to shoot. Direction = from cue ball toward mouse.\n\nLet me do: \n- Mouse move: aim line from cue ball toward mouse cursor.\n- Mouse down: start charging (power oscillates or increases).\n- Mouse up: shoot with current power in aim direction.\n\nI'll make power increase while holding, capped, maybe oscillate for skill. Let me make it increase and cap — simpler and predictable. Actually oscillating is more fun. I'll do increasing with a max, and show a power bar.\n\nCue ball placement (ball in hand): when it's a foul or break, show a \"place the cue ball\" mode — click to place behind head string.\n\nLet me write it. I'll keep the code organized.\n\nLet me start writing the file.\n",
